Can you determine evidences of port scan activity?

To solve it I will be looking for TCP Reset (RST) flags and specific ICMP error messages is the core way to identify port
scan evidence.

1. What the TCP RST (Reset) Flag Tells You
When an attacker scans a port using a standard TCP SYN scan (stealth scan), they send a SYN packet to a port.
How the target responds with a RST tells the attacker if the port is open or closed.
Evidence of a Closed Port: The attacker sends SYN → Your server replies with RST, ACK.
Why this is evidence: A healthy server immediately rejects traffic to a closed port by sending a Reset. 
If you see thousands of RST, ACK packets leaving your server toward a single external IP, your server is screaming,
"Stop talking to me on these closed ports!"
Evidence of an Open Port: The attacker sends SYN → Your server replies with SYN, ACK (Open!) → The attacker immediately 
replies with RST.
Why this is evidence: The attacker doesn't want to establish a real connection (which logs their identity on the 
                      application). They just want to know it's open, so they break the connection immediately using a 
                      RST packet.

2. What ICMP Packets Tell You (UDP & Firewall Scanning)
TCP isn't the only protocol scanned. When attackers scan UDP ports or run into a Firewall, ICMP packets are generated as 
evidence.
UDP Port Scanning (ICMP Type 3, Code 3): UDP is connectionless, so closed ports don't send a TCP Reset. 
Instead, if an attacker sends a UDP packet to a closed port, your operating system replies with an ICMP Destination
Unreachable (Port Unreachable) message.
The Evidence: A massive spike in outbound ICMP Type 3 Code 3 messages from your server to an outside IP means someone is
              mapping your UDP ports.
Firewall / Filtered Ports (ICMP Type 3, Code 13): If your firewall is configured to actively reject blocked traffic rather
than silently dropping it, it will send an ICMP Communication Administratively Prohibited message back to the scanner.
The Evidence: High volumes of ICMP Type 3 Code 13 indicate an attacker hitting a wall of filtered/protected ports.

1. The TCP Filters
To find the TCP Resets you want to filter for packets where the Reset (RST) flag is explicitly set to 1.
     To see the target system rejecting connections (Closed Ports):
      tcp.flags.reset == 1 && tcp.flags.ack == 1
(This shows the target telling the scanner: "This port is closed, stop trying.")
     To see the scanner tearing down open connections (Open Ports):
      tcp.flags.reset == 1 && tcp.flags.ack == 0
(This shows the scanner abruptly dropping the connection the moment it finds an open port.)
     To see the incoming scan attempts (The SYN Flood):
      tcp.flags.syn == 1 && tcp.flags.ack == 0

2. The UDP / ICMP Filters
Because UDP does not have flags like TCP, you must look for the ICMP error messages generated when a scanner hits a
closed UDP port or a firewall.
     To see UDP Port Unreachable errors (Closed UDP Ports):
      icmp.type == 3 && icmp.code == 3
(This is the ultimate proof of a UDP port scan. You will see a flood of these sent from your target server back to the
attacker.)
     To see Firewall Rejections (Filtered Ports):
      icmp.type == 3 && icmp.code == 13
(This shows your firewall actively blocking and turning away the scanner's packets.)
     To catch general ICMP Network Sweeping (Ping Sweeps):
      icmp.type == 8
(This filters for ICMP Echo Requests, which attackers use to see if a machine is online before scanning its ports.)

Pro-Tip:Combining Them
If you want to look at both TCP Resets and UDP ICMP errors at the exact same time in your Wireshark window, you can 
combine them using the || (OR) operator:
      tcp.flags.reset == 1 || (icmp.type == 3 && icmp.code == 3)
If you apply this combined filter and see the packet list instantly fill up with thousands of rows switching rapidly
between different port numbers, you have definitive proof of an active scan.
